From 8086417a402b91d8ee905f4621d95d4abf6e00eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 07:05:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][AudioEngine] A correction of the sync of passthrough
 sound lands as close as its packets allow

The A/V offset that the bridge measures at the output of the PC ("the
picture went out against its sound by X ms") was steady within a video
but different from one video to the next, and stepped after a pause:
-26 ms of it after one start of TrueHD, -15 after another, -13 after a
resume, +11 and +22 after starts ahead of the video.

A passthrough stream is put in sync by pauses (ahead) or by packets left
out (behind), and SyncStream() ended a correction as soon as the error
was within 30 ms, keeping what was left until the error passed the
threshold of 100 ms; a resume from a pause was put back only from
30 ms. A pause is as long as the error, so ahead the stream can land
within a millisecond; a packet is left out whole, so behind within half
a packet (TrueHD 10 ms, E-AC-3 16 ms, DTS 5 ms).

ActiveAESyncLanding.h: RawLanded() is where a correction of a
passthrough stream ends, and RawResumeCheckMs() where a resume is put
back (where a correction lands, at least 10 ms, the noise of the
average). The errors of TrueHD are measured scaled
(TRUEHD_SYNC_ERROR_SCALE) and the lengths are scaled as they are. PCM
and transcoded sound keep their 30 ms. No more cuts in the sound than
before: the pauses and packets of a correction are the same cut, a few
milliseconds longer; a resume within 10 ms of the video is left alone.
The rest of the offset that the bridge measures is the display latency
of the first video at a new rate, which Hades measures and takes from
the next video on ("the frames are chosen 1 refresh period later"); the
latency of the TV and of the sound bar are not seen from the PC.
